1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,901
This is the LLM Powered File System Assistant, built for the Airtribe
assignment.

2
00:00:03,901 --> 00:00:09,006
Ask a question in plain English, inspect the file tool calls, and get an
answer based on document content.

3
00:00:09,006 --> 00:00:12,763
The examples in this video were generated by live Claude Opus five point
five.

4
00:00:12,763 --> 00:00:15,653
Waiting time is shortened, and the file operations are real.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,189
Part A is implemented in fs tools dot py.

6
00:00:20,189 --> 00:00:22,164
Read file extracts text and metadata.

7
00:00:22,164 --> 00:00:24,566
List files discovers the available documents.

8
00:00:24,566 --> 00:00:27,823
Search in file returns case insensitive matches with context.

9
00:00:27,823 --> 00:00:30,865
Write file creates a text output and any missing folders.

10
00:00:30,865 --> 00:00:35,189
The sample set contains eight fictional resumes across PDF, Word, and plain
text.

11
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,740
Part B describes these functions with strict JSON schemas.

12
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:42,291
The program sends the question and tools to Anthropic.

13
00:00:42,291 --> 00:00:46,022
It executes complete tool calls locally and returns all their results
together.

14
00:00:46,022 --> 00:00:50,699
The entire assistant response, including signed thinking blocks, is
preserved for the next request.

15
00:00:50,699 --> 00:00:53,203
The loop stops when Claude provides the final answer.

16
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,005
The first required query asks the assistant to read all resumes.

17
00:00:58,005 --> 00:01:01,949
The log shows a directory listing, followed by one read for each of the
eight files.

18
00:01:01,949 --> 00:01:05,704
PDF page counts appear in the results, including Priya Sharma’s two page
resume.

19
00:01:05,704 --> 00:01:09,601
The answer covers the complete set rather than guessing names or skipping a
format.

20
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,506
The second query searches for Python experience.

21
00:01:18,506 --> 00:01:24,094
All eight resumes are checked, including different capitalizations and
phrases such as Django slash Python.

22
00:01:24,094 --> 00:01:29,367
Four candidates have professional experience: John Doe, Priya Sharma, Elena
Rodriguez, and David Kim.

23
00:01:29,367 --> 00:01:34,015
Sofia Novak has a Monty Python hobby, so a text match alone is not enough
to qualify her.

24
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,408
The third query asks for a summary of John Doe’s PDF.

25
00:01:39,408 --> 00:01:43,043
The assistant reads the resume and calls write file with the generated
Markdown.

26
00:01:43,043 --> 00:01:46,996
The program creates the summaries directory and reports the destination and
byte count.

27
00:01:46,996 --> 00:01:51,404
The saved file is actual output from that tool call, rather than text
displayed only in the chat.

28
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:57,468
Here is the generated summary.

29
00:01:57,468 --> 00:02:02,069
It captures John’s backend engineering experience, key skills, work
highlights, and education.

30
00:02:02,069 --> 00:02:03,684
The original resume stays intact.

31
00:02:03,684 --> 00:02:07,990
Existing files are replaced only when the assistant created them, and
writes are atomic.

32
00:02:07,990 --> 00:02:12,786
A committed copy of the example is kept separately so new users can run the
default summary query.

33
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,126
Missing files return structured errors rather than crashing the
conversation.

34
00:02:19,126 --> 00:02:22,931
Tools refuse outside paths, hidden credentials, and executable outputs.

35
00:02:22,931 --> 00:02:30,327
The test suite also covers corrupt documents, encrypted files, symlink
races, incomplete tool calls, conversation reset, and API failures.

36
00:02:30,327 --> 00:02:34,989
Two hundred and fifty seven tests pass, with ninety four percent overall
line coverage.

37
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,153
To run the project, install the pinned requirements and set your Anthropic
API key.

38
00:02:41,153 --> 00:02:43,455
Multi workspace keys also need a workspace ID.

39
00:02:43,455 --> 00:02:46,257
Start the interactive chat, or pass a question directly.

40
00:02:46,257 --> 00:02:52,361
The repository includes the three example queries, captured transcripts,
the generated summary, and the validation report.

41
00:02:52,361 --> 00:02:54,863
Every file action remains visible in the tool log.
